      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.4.03-02",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Geothermal energy uses heat from within the Earth. In suitable settings, hot fluids or heated rock can provide usable heat and sometimes electricity generation. For electricity, the system must deliver heat capable of driving the appropriate generating process. Favourable geology, accessible depth, water or fluid arrangements and drilling costs influence feasibility. Iceland, a volcanic island, is a well-known example: in 2003 its government reported that about 88% of homes were heated with geothermal water and that geothermal power stations supplied 18% of its electricity. A hot climate at the surface is not evidence of a geothermal reservoir; solar heating and internal Earth heat are different sources.",
      "claim_ids": [
